Clockwork Obsidian

Creative Catalyst: The resonant, metallic warmth of the music is derived from oscillators hand-cast from fallen meteorite iron and scavenged 18th-century escapement gears.

This genre is a cold-burn fusion of fractured, high-speed rhythmic complexity and the velvet, melancholy weight of late-night soul. It sounds like a decaying mainframe dreaming of a smoky jazz club; deep, sub-bass techno pulses are constantly interrupted by the stuttering, unpredictable clockwork of IDM, all smoothed over by the rich, tactile warmth of a Rhodes MK1. It is the sound of high-tech solitude, where the precision of the future meets the soulful, mechanical imperfection of the past.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 110–130 BPM; a "limping" syncopated pulse that frequently shifts between straight 4/4 and jagged, micro-rhythmic glitch swings.
*   Harmonic Palette: Minor 7th and 9th chords, jazz-inflected extensions, and atonal, metallic dissonance created by resonant filter feedback.
*   Production Signatures: 1) Tape-saturated Rhodes chords processed through bit-crushed granular delay; 2) Micro-edited percussion samples that mimic the sound of ticking clockwork; 3) Heavy use of side-chain compression that "ducks" the soul elements to the erratic rhythm of the meteorite-oscillator kick.
*   Dynamics: A claustrophobic, oscillating build where dense, complex rhythmic sections suddenly open into sparse, melancholic Rhodes-led "air pockets" before collapsing back into mechanical chaos.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement emerged in the subterranean "Gear-Vaults" of post-industrial Riga, where a collective of data-miners and horologists began modifying antique synthesizers with fragments of the Tunguska meteorite. These artisans believe the iron holds a residual, non-human frequency that regulates the "truth" of a machine. Their rituals involve late-night listening sessions in rooms lined with copper mesh, where they dress in matte-black utilitarian wool and brass-filigreed optics. It is a subculture obsessed with the "persistence of memory in hardware," viewing their music as a way to mourn the obsolescence of human touch in a digitized, dystopic landscape.

Typical Instruments: Rhodes MK1 (heavily modified), meteorite-iron oscillator modules, antique clockwork trigger-sequencers, granular sampling engines.

For Fans Of: Autechre, Boards of Canada, Miles Davis (late 70s period), Oneohtrix Point Never.

